<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carloman (mayor of the palace)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 710–754</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carloman was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> and Frankish mayor of the palace in Austrasia from 741 to 747, ruling jointly with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>. He campaigned against the Alemanni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, crushing Alemannic resistance at Cannstatt in 746, and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>'s reform of the Frankish church. In 747 he renounced power and became a monk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at Monte Soracte and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carloman was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, mayor of the palace and duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and his wife Chrotrud of Treves. When Charles died in 741, Carloman and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> succeeded to their father's positions, Carloman in Austrasia and Pepin in Neustria. Charles had left nothing to his half-brother Grifo, the son of his second wife Swanachild. When Grifo demanded a share, the brothers besieged him at Laon in 741, took him captive and forced him into a monastery.</p><p>On Carloman's initiative the two mayors installed the Merovingian Childeric III as king in 743, though the throne had been vacant since the death of Theuderic IV in 737. For seven years the brothers worked together and campaigned jointly against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunald-i-of-aquitaine">Hunald I of Aquitaine</a>, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in 742 and 743, and against Odilo, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, their brother-in-law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In his realm Carloman supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Winfrid</a>, later Saint Boniface, whom he charged with restructuring the church in Austrasia. In 742 he helped convene the Concilium Germanicum, the first major synod in the eastern Frankish lands, which he chaired jointly with Boniface. It ruled that priests might not bear arms or keep women in their houses and that they must root out pagan belief. Carloman also gave the land for the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>, one of Boniface's most important foundations.</p><p>He could also be ruthless. After repeated revolts, in 746 he summoned the Alemanni magnates to an assembly at Cannstatt and had most of them, said to number in the thousands, arrested and executed for high treason in the Blood Court at Cannstatt. This destroyed almost the whole tribal leadership and ended the independence of the duchy of Alemannia, thereafter governed by counts appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 15 August 747 Carloman renounced his position and was tonsured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-zachary">Pope Zachary</a>. The sources agree that his withdrawal was voluntary, though some have speculated that Pepin asked the pope to keep him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He founded a monastery on Monte Soratte and then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>. In 754 he left it at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> to ask Pepin, now king, not to march on Italy. Pepin refused, and Carloman died soon afterwards at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> on 17 August and was buried at Monte Cassino. Norman Cantor called him the first of a new type of saintly king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carloman (mayor of the palace)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 746 <b>Carloman</b> summoned the Alemannic nobility to Cannstatt and executed many of them, ending the ducal office.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-alamannia">Duchy of Alamannia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vienne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Vienne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.53,4.88&amp;year=738" aria-label="Vienne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4666 558 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carloman (mayor of the palace)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5116 828h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5116 828h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.53,4.88&amp;year=738">What was here in 738 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
